description:
The fix is usually to unset the scopes and set it to not in progress. This has happened for a long time. Jobs have a  90 day TTL, but even the oldest TTL documents suggest that the jobs have erroneously ended up both in progress and completed since January, even though the code that marks jobs complete explicitly sets in prog to false. Other jobs also have this issue (e.g. building container job).

{noformat}
db.evg.service.jobs.updateOne({"_id": "distro-scheduler.rhel80-small.2021-05-11.19-07-00"}, {"$unset": {"scopes": 1}, "$set": {"status.in_prog": false}}){noformat}

[Splunk logs for the job|https://mongodb.splunkcloud.com/en-US/app/search/search?s=%2FservicesNS%2Fnobody%2Fsearch%2Fsaved%2Fsearches%2FEvergreen%253A%2520Error%2520rate%2520in%2520production&display.page.search.mode=fast&dispatch.sample_ratio=1&workload_pool=&q=search%20index%3Devergreen%20distro-scheduler.rhel80-small.2021-05-11.19-07-00&earliest=1620760080&latest=1620760320&sid=1620772448.837608] suggest that it occurs when a deploy goes out. Since the deploy cancels all running jobs, it can cause some jobs to fail to be marked complete due to context cancellation. They're supposed to re-dispatch if they're in progress and stale, but in this case, it seems that marking it complete succeeded, but the dispatcher still attempted to dispatch the job (probably because it was still in progress at the moment that the dispatcher was trying to re-dispatch the stale job and the main thread was trying to mark it complete).
